+# Sleep Options Brief
+
+## Situation
+
+This brief is for a primary-care conversation about a 67-year-old man who has had trouble falling asleep and waking during the night for about three months. He takes apixaban 5 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation. Three months is the duration threshold used in current descriptions of chronic insomnia when sleep difficulty also occurs often enough and is not better explained by another problem; only a clinician can determine whether that diagnosis fits him ([NHLBI](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/insomnia)).
+
+The aim here is education, not a diagnosis or a personalized treatment choice. He should not start, stop, or change apixaban, a sleep aid, a supplement, or any dose based on this brief. The useful next step is a medication and sleep review with his clinician, including how often symptoms occur, daytime effects, schedule, caffeine and alcohol, snoring or gasping, restless legs, mood, pain, and other medicines or supplements. A one- to two-week sleep diary can give that visit concrete information ([NHLBI](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/insomnia/diagnosis)).
+
+## Comparison
+
+| Option | What the evidence says and likely benefit | Important limitations | Age and medication considerations |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| CBT-I | Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is the recommended initial or first-line treatment for chronic insomnia. It combines cognitive work with behavioral components such as stimulus control and sleep restriction; it can improve falling asleep and staying asleep without adding a drug ([ACP guideline](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27136449/); [NHLBI](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/insomnia/treatment)). | It takes active participation over multiple sessions, and access can vary. Sleep restriction is structured time-in-bed therapy, not simply getting less sleep, and should be individualized by a trained clinician when safety issues or other conditions are present. Sleep hygiene alone is not equivalent to the full treatment ([2025 VA/DoD patient guide](https://www.healthquality.va.gov/guidelines/CD/insomnia/CST-01-Treatment-of-Insomnia-Provider-Patient-Guide-Final-508.pdf)). | It avoids adding medication-interaction and anticholinergic burdens, an advantage for an older adult using an anticoagulant. Ask about in-person, telehealth, or validated digital CBT-I and whether his history changes how its components should be used. |
+| Melatonin | Evidence is mixed and outcome-specific. Reviews suggest possible small improvement in time to fall asleep, but not consistent improvement in sleep quality or time awake at night; major guidelines have found insufficient evidence or advised against it for chronic insomnia ([NCCIH sleep review](https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/sleep-disorders-and-complementary-health-approaches)). | Short-term use appears relatively safe for most adults, but long-term safety is not established. Possible effects include sleepiness, headache, dizziness, and nausea, and supplement contents may differ from the label ([NCCIH melatonin fact sheet](https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/melatonin-what-you-need-to-know)). | NCCIH specifically advises people taking blood thinner medicines to use melatonin only with medical supervision and notes it may remain active longer in older people. That is a reason for clinician and pharmacist review, not proof of a specific apixaban interaction or a reason to alter apixaban. No dose is proposed here. |
+| Diphenhydramine / doxylamine | These sedating first-generation antihistamines may cause drowsiness, but current guidance does not support them as a durable chronic-insomnia strategy. The 2025 VA/DoD patient summary advises against OTC diphenhydramine for insomnia because long-term effectiveness is poor and side effects occur ([VA/DoD summary](https://www.healthquality.va.gov/guidelines/CD/insomnia/I-OSA-2025-Patient-Summary_final_20250205.pdf)). | Both are strongly anticholinergic. The 2023 AGS Beers Criteria says to avoid them in older adults because of confusion, dry mouth, constipation, reduced clearance, and tolerance as hypnotics; cumulative anticholinergic exposure is associated with falls, delirium, and dementia ([AGS Beers Criteria](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12478568/)). | At 67, the older-adult warning applies. Sedation, impaired attention, and falls matter especially because a fall or injury can be more consequential while anticoagulated. That is a clinical-risk inference, not a claimed direct drug-drug interaction with apixaban. Ask the clinician to review all combination cold/nighttime products to avoid accidental duplication. |
+
+## Safety
+
+Apixaban prevents clots but can cause serious, potentially fatal bleeding. Its official label also warns that other products can alter exposure or add bleeding risk, so the clinician and pharmacist need a complete list of prescriptions, OTC products, and supplements ([DailyMed official label](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/getFile.cfm?setid=a454cd24-0c6d-46e8-b1e4-197388606175&type=pdf)). This brief does not assert that melatonin or either antihistamine directly changes apixaban levels. Instead, the relevant established concerns differ: NCCIH calls for supervision when melatonin is combined with a blood thinner, while sedating anticholinergics can contribute to confusion and falls.
+
+Before choosing an insomnia approach, the visit should screen for causes or mimics that may need different care: medication timing or adverse effects, sleep apnea symptoms, restless legs, pain, depression or anxiety, alcohol, caffeine, and an irregular sleep schedule. NHLBI notes that a sleep study may be used when another sleep disorder such as sleep apnea is suspected ([NHLBI diagnosis guidance](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/insomnia/diagnosis)).
+
+The apixaban Medication Guide says to get medical help right away for unexpected, severe, or uncontrolled bleeding and to tell the prescriber about a fall or head injury ([DailyMed](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/getFile.cfm?setid=a454cd24-0c6d-46e8-b1e4-197388606175&type=pdf)). These cautions support appropriate care; they do not replace instructions already given by his own clinicians.
+
+## Questions for clinician
+
+1. Does his pattern meet criteria for chronic insomnia, and what underlying medical, medication, mood, circadian, or sleep-disorder causes should be assessed?
+2. Would a sleep diary or screening for sleep apnea or restless legs help before selecting treatment?
+3. Is CBT-I appropriate, and which accessible in-person, telehealth, or validated digital program would you recommend?
+4. If melatonin is considered, how do his age, apixaban use, other medicines, and supplement quality affect its risks and expected benefit?
+5. Should he avoid diphenhydramine, doxylamine, and combination “nighttime” products given their anticholinergic and sedating effects?
+6. Which symptoms or bleeding events should trigger an urgent call or emergency care while he is taking apixaban?
+
+## Sources
+
+- American College of Physicians, *Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder in Adults* clinical practice guideline: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27136449/
+- National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute, *Insomnia Treatment* and diagnosis guidance: https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/insomnia/treatment and https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/insomnia/diagnosis
+- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health, *Melatonin: What You Need To Know*: https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/melatonin-what-you-need-to-know
+- U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs and Department of Defense, 2025 insomnia guideline patient materials: https://www.healthquality.va.gov/guidelines/CD/insomnia/index.asp
+- American Geriatrics Society, 2023 updated Beers Criteria: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12478568/
+- DailyMed, official ELIQUIS (apixaban) prescribing information: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/getFile.cfm?setid=a454cd24-0c6d-46e8-b1e4-197388606175&type=pdf
